new_dynarec arm64: run on the Nintendo Switch (Horizon, libnx)

All changes are under __SWITCH__ (set by builder/build-switch.sh); other builds are
unchanged. The mechanism is the one mupen64plus-libretro-nx uses (its HAVE_LIBNX
blocks), on top of the DOUBLE_CACHE_ADDR mode the core already has.

1. Code cache. Horizon never maps a page writable and executable at once and has
   no mmap. The executable view (base_addr_rx) is the 32 MB znx_jit_memory array
   that switch/jit_memory.S places in the program's .text; the writable view
   (base_addr) is a second mapping of the same pages made with svcMapProcessMemory
   (znx_jit_map in switch/jit_switch.c). Because the cache is part of the image,
   generated code reaches the C helpers with direct branches and the device state
   with adrp wherever the NRO is loaded, as it does with extra_memory elsewhere.
   extra_memory itself is no longer needed and shrinks to one page.

2. Instruction cache. cache_flush uses libnx's armDCacheFlush on the writable view
   and armICacheInvalidate on the executable view: the same "dc civac"/"ic ivau"
   loops, with the thread's cache-maintenance flag set around them as the Horizon
   kernel expects.

Deliberately not changed:
- x18. It stays allocatable and stays the trampoline register (EXCLUDE_REG 29,
  TRAMPOLINE_REG 18, as on Linux). Unlike Darwin, Horizon keeps x18 across
  context switches and nothing reserves it: devkitA64's GCC allocates it as an
  ordinary caller-saved register (libnx.a itself uses it in several hundred
  instructions), and mupen64plus-libretro-nx runs this recompiler with x18 in use.
- stop_after_jal. libretro-nx sets it to 0 on the Switch; RMG-K's value (1) is
  kept, so blocks are cut exactly where the PC and Mac builds cut them.

--- a/mupen64plus-core/src/device/r4300/new_dynarec/arm64/assem_arm64.c
+++ b/mupen64plus-core/src/device/r4300/new_dynarec/arm64/assem_arm64.c
@@ -83,6 +83,11 @@
 #if defined(__APPLE__)
 #include <libkern/OSCacheControl.h>
 #endif
+#if defined(__SWITCH__)
+/* libnx (switch/arm/cache.h), declared here so the core does not need the libnx headers. */
+void armDCacheFlush(void* addr, size_t size);
+void armICacheInvalidate(void* addr, size_t size);
+#endif
 
 static uintptr_t literals[1024][2];
 static unsigned int needs_clear_cache[1<<(TARGET_SIZE_2-17)];
@@ -269,6 +274,15 @@
 {
 #if defined(__APPLE__)
     sys_icache_invalidate(start, (size_t)(end - start));
+#elif defined(__SWITCH__)
+    // Callers pass addresses in the executable view. The code was written through the
+    // writable view: push those lines out of the data cache there, then drop the stale
+    // instructions at the address they are executed from. libnx's routines are the same
+    // "dc civac"/"ic ivau" loops as below, and also raise the thread's cache-maintenance
+    // flag that the Horizon kernel expects around them.
+    size_t size = (size_t)(end - start);
+    armDCacheFlush(start - (char*)base_addr_rx + (char*)base_addr, size);
+    armICacheInvalidate(start, size);
 #elif !defined(WIN32)
     // Don't rely on GCC's __clear_cache implementation, as it caches
     // icache/dcache cache line sizes, that can vary between cores on
